// Miller code - Hello World! :: RAiNBOW_Hello-World
//   colour     : not assigned by Linguist
//   layer      : full (byte-balanced rainbow bar)
//   upstream   : https://github.com/quxianp/RAiNBOW_Hello-World
//   reference  : see https://esolangs.org/ and https://rosettacode.org/

# Miller code puts a transition at the *end* of a bit rather than the
# middle, and none at all in the middle of a repeated bit. It needs no
# polarity, which Manchester does. Below are the bit pairs Miller would
# emit for the ASCII of "Hello World!", where a dash is a steady level:
#
# H = 01001000 -> 01 -1 01 -1 00 1- 00 1-
# e = 01100101 -> 01 -1 01 1- 00 1- 01
#
# The encoding needs a clock and a start bit; without one a decoder
# cannot tell a run of identical bits from a longer one.
